from nodelib import *
import qn
from fractions import Fraction

def frac(x):
    if x.denominator == 1: return '%+d' % x.numerator if x != 0 else '0'
    return '%+d/%d' % (x.numerator, x.denominator)

HAD = {h['id']: h for h in D['hadrons']}
PRO, NEU = D['composites']
ALL_PARTS = ['sm_' + p['id'] for p in D['particles']]

N('sm_overview', 'The Standard Model', 'concept',
  'The tested description of every known elementary particle and three of the four forces: seventeen particles, one framework, and a list of what it cannot explain.',
  '<p>Everything you can touch, and nearly everything experiments have made, is built from a short list of <b>elementary particles</b>: the smallest things we have found, with no measured parts. Twelve of them are matter particles (six quarks and six leptons, in three similar families called generations), four carry the forces (the gluon, the photon, the W and the Z), and one, the Higgs boson, is connected to how the others get their mass.</p><p>The count of seventeen is a convention. The W⁺ and W⁻ are counted as one particle, the eight gluons as one, and antiparticles, colour states and spin states are not counted separately; counted differently, the number would be larger.</p><p>Ordinary matter needs only three of them: the up quark, the down quark and the electron. Quarks make protons and neutrons; protons, neutrons and electrons make atoms.</p><p><b>The picture on this level is not a place.</b> It is the list of everything matter and the forces (except gravity) are made of, as far as experiments have reached.</p>',
  '<p>The Standard Model is a quantum field theory built on the symmetry group SU(3) × SU(2) × U(1): SU(3) for the strong force, and SU(2) × U(1) for the electroweak force, which at low energy separates into electromagnetism and the weak force. It makes predictions that have been tested over a huge range of energies.</p><p>It has a known gap: gravity is not in it. And it needs about 19 numbers (masses, mixing angles, the strengths of the forces) that must be measured rather than predicted.</p>',
  '<p>The Lagrangian has four parts: gauge-field kinetic terms, fermion kinetic terms with gauge couplings, the Higgs doublet\'s kinetic and potential terms, and Yukawa couplings of the Higgs to fermions. The Higgs field\'s nonzero vacuum value v ≈ 246 GeV (PDG) breaks SU(2) × U(1) to U(1)em and gives masses to W, Z and the charged fermions. Neutrino masses and mixing require an extension.</p><p>All values in this explorer come from the Particle Data Group\'s Review of Particle Physics, 2026 edition (F. Takahashi et al., Int. J. Mod. Phys. A 41, 2630011).</p>',
  [['Elementary particles', '17: 6 quarks, 6 leptons, 4 gauge bosons (gluon, photon, W, Z), 1 Higgs boson. Convention: W⁺ and W⁻ count as one, the 8 gluons as one, and antiparticles and colour states are not counted separately'], ['Forces included', 'strong, electromagnetic, weak (and the Higgs field)'], ['Not included', 'gravity; dark matter; dark energy; a mechanism for neutrino masses'], ['Free parameters', 'about 19 (a counting convention; see "What the Standard Model leaves out")'], ['Data source', 'PDG Review of Particle Physics, 2026']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.5'), ('UP3', '11.1'), ('HSP', '23.1')), ['standard_model', 'quarks_leptons'], parents=[], parts=ALL_PARTS + ['sm_proton', 'sm_neutron', 'sm_hadrons', 'sm_interactions', 'sm_quantum_fields'],
  oq=['Gravity, dark matter, dark energy and the matter–antimatter imbalance are not explained by the Standard Model.'],
  viz={'type': 'blocks', 'nodes': [
      {'id': 'q', 'label': 'quarks: u d  c s  t b', 'x': 16, 'y': 18, 'color': 'accent'}, {'id': 'l', 'label': 'leptons: e μ τ + 3 neutrinos', 'x': 16, 'y': 62, 'color': 'accent'},
      {'id': 'g', 'label': 'gluon, photon, W, Z', 'x': 62, 'y': 18, 'color': 'gold'}, {'id': 'h', 'label': 'Higgs boson', 'x': 62, 'y': 62, 'color': 'pink'},
      {'id': 'x', 'label': 'gravity: not in the model', 'x': 62, 'y': 100, 'color': 'dim'}],
      'edges': [{'from': 'g', 'to': 'q'}, {'from': 'g', 'to': 'l'}, {'from': 'h', 'to': 'q'}, {'from': 'h', 'to': 'l'}],
      'caption': 'The Standard Model at a glance: matter particles (blue), force carriers (gold) and the Higgs (pink). Gravity is left out. A map of ideas, not a picture of anything.'})

N('sm_quantum_fields', 'Quantum fields: what a particle is', 'field',
  'In the Standard Model a particle is not a tiny ball: it is the smallest possible ripple in a field that fills all of space.',
  '<p>It is natural to picture an electron as a tiny ball. The Standard Model describes it differently: there is an <b>electron field</b> everywhere in space, and an electron is a single, indivisible ripple in that field. In this picture every electron in the universe is the same because each is a ripple in one field. The same goes for every other particle: quarks, photons, the Higgs. Each kind has its own field.</p><p>This is why all electrons are identical, and why particles can be created and destroyed (a ripple can start or stop) while the fields stay.</p><p>The "ball" picture is a handy approximation when a particle is far from others; the field picture is the language the theory is written in. What that language says about what is really there is still argued (see the open question).</p>',
  '<p>A field has a value at every point. In quantum theory the ways a field can vibrate come in steps: the smallest step is one particle, the next is two, and so on. That is what "quantum" means here: a field cannot hold half a particle.</p><p>Nothing here has a definite position until it is measured, so "a ripple" does not mean a hump with edges. It means something that, when probed, shows up as one particle with a certain probability at a certain place.</p>',
  '<p>In quantum field theory the state space is a Fock space built from a vacuum by creation operators; particle number is not conserved in general, but charges (like electric charge) are. Interactions appear as terms coupling different fields. Perturbation theory organises calculations by Feynman diagrams (see that card); the diagram\'s internal lines are terms in the series, not trajectories.</p>',
  [['Fields in the Standard Model', '12 matter fields (6 quarks, 6 leptons), 4 kinds of force field (gluon, photon, W/Z), 1 Higgs field'], ['Why all electrons are identical', 'in quantum field theory, each is an excitation of the same electron field']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.5'), ('CPH', '29.5')), ['standard_model', 'wave_particle', 'photon_node'],
  viz={'type': 'plot', 'x': [-6, 6], 'y': [-1.6, 1.6], 'fns': [{'f': 'a*gauss(x,0,w)*cos(4*x-3*t)', 'label': 'a ripple in a field', 'color': 'accent', 'width': 2}],
       'params': [{'id': 'a', 'label': 'strength of the ripple', 'min': 0.2, 'max': 1.5, 'value': 1}, {'id': 'w', 'label': 'width of the ripple', 'min': 0.5, 'max': 3, 'value': 1.2}],
       'caption': 'Schematic, in one dimension: a field\'s value across space, with a single localised ripple travelling along it. A real particle is not a visible wave: this only illustrates the idea that a particle is an excitation of a field. Not to scale and not a measurement.'},
  oq=['What is the best interpretation of quantum fields? The mathematics is settled and tested; what it says about reality is still argued.'])

N('sm_interactions', 'The four interactions', 'field',
  'Four ways particles act on each other: strong, electromagnetic, weak and gravity; the Standard Model includes the first three.',
  '<p>Everything that happens, from a falling apple to a star burning, comes from four interactions. The <b>strong</b> force binds quarks into protons and neutrons. The <b>electromagnetic</b> force acts between electric charges and makes light. The <b>weak</b> force changes particles into other particles (it makes radioactive beta decay and powers the Sun\'s slow fusion). <b>Gravity</b> pulls mass together.</p><p>The first three are carried by particles: gluons, photons, and the W and Z. Gravity is described by Einstein\'s general relativity, which is not part of the Standard Model.</p>',
  '<p>An interaction can be thought of as a coupling between a kind of "charge" and a force-carrying field. For the strong force the charge is colour; for electromagnetism it is electric charge; for the weak force it is weak isospin (the W couples to it; the Z couples to a mix of weak isospin and electric charge). The Higgs field is different: its coupling to each particle is proportional to that particle\'s mass, and that coupling is where rest mass comes from.</p>',
  '<p>The three Standard Model forces are gauge interactions: local symmetries SU(3)c, SU(2)L and U(1)Y whose gauge bosons (8 gluons, W¹,²,³, B) mediate the forces. After the Higgs field acquires a vacuum value, W³ and B mix into the massless photon and the massive Z.</p>',
  [['Strong', 'carrier: gluon · couples to colour charge · acts on quarks and gluons'], ['Electromagnetic', 'carrier: photon · couples to electric charge · long range'], ['Weak', 'carriers: W⁺, W⁻, Z · couples to weak isospin (W) and a mix of weak isospin and charge (Z) · range about 2.5×10⁻¹⁸ m'], ['Gravity', 'not in the Standard Model · acts on all energy']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.5'), ('CPH', '33.2'), ('HSP', '23.1')), ['standard_model', 'weak_force_note'], parts=['sm_strong_force', 'sm_higgs_mechanism', 'sm_gravity'], related=['sm_photon', 'sm_w_boson', 'sm_z_boson', 'sm_gluon'],
  viz={'type': 'table', 'cols': ['Interaction', 'Carrier', 'Acts on', 'Range'], 'rows': [
      ['Strong', 'gluon (8)', 'quarks, gluons', 'quarks and gluons are confined; leftover force between hadrons about 1.4×10⁻¹⁵ m'], ['Electromagnetic', 'photon', 'electrically charged particles', 'unlimited'], ['Weak', 'W⁺, W⁻, Z', 'all quarks and leptons', 'about 2.5×10⁻¹⁸ m'], ['Gravity', 'no tested quantum carrier', 'everything', 'unlimited']]})

N('sm_strong_force', 'The strong force', 'field',
  'The strongest known force: it holds quarks inside protons and neutrons, and its leftover holds nuclei together.',
  '<p>The strong force acts on quarks. It is carried by gluons, and the "charge" it acts on is called colour, a name that has nothing to do with the colours you see. It is so strong that quarks are never found alone: pulling two apart takes so much energy that the energy turns into a new quark–antiquark pair instead.</p><p>Inside a proton the quarks are held by it. What holds protons and neutrons together in nuclei is a residual of the strong force: it reaches only to neighbouring particles, and at that short range it is strong enough to beat the electric repulsion between the protons. It is a leftover in the same way that the attraction between neutral molecules is a leftover of electric forces, not in being weak.</p>',
  '<p>The strong force is described by quantum chromodynamics (QCD). Its strength, written αs, depends on the energy of the process: it is about 0.118 at the energy of the Z boson but grows large at and below about 1 GeV (PDG QCD review); the proton, about 10⁻¹⁵ m across, is a system in this strongly coupled region. Because the coupling is strong at proton scales, calculations there need supercomputer "lattice" methods.</p>',
  '<p>QCD is the SU(3) gauge theory of colour with quarks in the triplet representation. Its colour factors are C_F = 4/3 and C_A = 3; the PDG quotes a measured C_A = 2.89 ± 0.03 (stat) ± 0.21 (syst) and C_F = 1.30 ± 0.01 ± 0.09, consistent with SU(3). Confinement is observed in lattice calculations but is not mathematically proved from the equations.</p>',
  [['Carrier', '8 gluons'], ['Charge it acts on', 'colour'], ['Coupling at the Z mass', 'αs ≈ 0.118 (PDG QCD review, world average 2026)'], ['Scale where it becomes strong', 'below about 1 GeV (PDG QCD review); ħc / 1 GeV is 0.2×10⁻¹⁵ m (my calculation), and a proton is about 10⁻¹⁵ m across, so the confinement scale is lower than 1 GeV']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.3'), ('HSP', '23.1')), ['qcd', 'nuclear_force'], parts=['sm_colour_confinement', 'sm_asymptotic_freedom', 'sm_residual_strong'],
  viz={'type': 'blocks', 'flow': True, 'nodes': [{'id': 'a', 'label': 'quark', 'x': 5, 'y': 50}, {'id': 'g', 'label': 'gluon', 'x': 50, 'y': 20, 'color': 'gold'}, {'id': 'b', 'label': 'quark', 'x': 95, 'y': 50}, {'id': 'c', 'label': 'gluon', 'x': 50, 'y': 85, 'color': 'gold'}],
       'edges': [{'from': 'a', 'to': 'g'}, {'from': 'g', 'to': 'b'}, {'from': 'b', 'to': 'c'}, {'from': 'c', 'to': 'a'}], 'caption': 'Quarks exchange gluons back and forth. Bookkeeping only: nothing literally travels along these lines, and "colour" is just a name for the strong force\'s kind of charge.'})

N('sm_colour_confinement', 'Colour charge and confinement', 'concept',
  'Quarks carry a kind of charge called colour, and only colourless combinations exist alone, so a quark can never be pulled out of a proton.',
  '<p>Quarks carry <b>colour charge</b>: one of three kinds, called red, green and blue as a loose analogy (they are not coloured). Just as electric charge can be positive or negative, a colour has a matching anticolour. In nature we only find combinations with no net colour: a baryon is one quark of each colour, a meson is a colour and its anticolour. This is why free quarks are never seen: <b>confinement</b>.</p><p>Try to pull a quark out of a proton and the energy stored in the stretched gluon field becomes enough to create a new quark–antiquark pair, giving a new particle instead of a free quark.</p>',
  '<p>"Colourless" means a combination that is unchanged by changing the labels, in the way a mix of red, green and blue light looks white. That is where the combination rules for hadrons (see "Hadrons") come from: three quarks, a quark and an antiquark, and (rarer) exotic combinations.</p><p>Colour also resolved a puzzle: the Δ⁺⁺ particle has three identical up quarks with parallel spins, which the exclusion principle forbids unless quarks have another property to tell them apart.</p>',
  '<p>Colour confinement follows from the strong coupling at long distances and is seen in lattice QCD as a linearly rising potential between a heavy quark and antiquark, with a "string" of colour field between them. It has not been derived analytically from the QCD equations; proving the associated mass gap is a Clay Millennium Prize problem.</p>',
  [['Colour charges', 'three (colour) and three (anticolour)'], ['Allowed combinations', 'colourless: three quarks, three antiquarks, or a quark and an antiquark'], ['A confined particle?', 'yes: no isolated quark or gluon has ever been observed']],
  'C2', (('UP3', '11.3'), ('CPH', '33.5')), ['qcd', 'quarks_leptons'],
  viz={'type': 'plot', 'x': [0.05, 3], 'y': [-4, 6], 'fns': [{'f': '-a/x + s*x', 'label': 'energy of a quark–antiquark pair', 'color': 'gold', 'width': 2.2}],
       'params': [{'id': 'a', 'label': 'short-range strength', 'min': 0.1, 'max': 1, 'value': 0.4, 'step': 0.05}, {'id': 's', 'label': 'long-range pull', 'min': 0.5, 'max': 3, 'value': 1.5, 'step': 0.1}],
       'xlabel': 'separation (arbitrary units)', 'ylabel': 'energy (arbitrary units)',
       'caption': 'A SCHEMATIC model, not a measurement: at short range the energy falls like a Coulomb attraction, and at long range it rises in proportion to the separation, so separating the pair costs ever more energy. The parameters are chosen to make the shape visible, not fitted to data.'},
  oq=['Confinement is seen in computer calculations of QCD but not proved from the equations (C2).'])

N('sm_asymptotic_freedom', 'Asymptotic freedom', 'concept',
  'The strong force gets weaker when quarks are very close or collide at high energy, which is why high-energy experiments can see quarks almost free.',
  '<p>Unlike electric forces, the strong force grows stronger as particles move apart and weaker as they get very close. At very high collision energies quarks inside a proton hardly feel each other and behave almost as free particles; this property is called <b>asymptotic freedom</b>. It is why scattering experiments at high energy can treat quarks as nearly independent points, and why calculations are possible there.</p>',
  '<p>The strength of the strong force is measured by a number αs that changes with the energy of the process: about 0.118 at the mass of the Z boson, smaller at higher energy, and large (around and above 1) near 1 GeV (PDG QCD review 2026: "αs ∼ 0.1 in the 0.1–1 TeV range, strongly interacting at and below 1 GeV"). The PDG notes that measurements of αs at many energies show this running exactly as QCD predicts.</p>',
  '<p>At one loop the running is αs(Q) = αs(M_Z) / [1 + (b₀ αs(M_Z) / 2π) ln(Q/M_Z)], with b₀ = 11 − 2n_f/3. With n_f = 5 flavours b₀ = 23/3. The figure uses this one-loop form, which captures the trend but is not precise (PDG results use higher-order calculations). The 2004 Nobel Prize in Physics went to Gross, Politzer and Wilczek for discovering asymptotic freedom.</p>',
  [['Coupling at the Z mass', 'αs(M_Z) ≈ 0.118'], ['Used in the figure', 'one-loop running, n_f = 5, valid roughly for Q from 5 GeV upwards'], ['Source', 'PDG 2026, Quantum Chromodynamics review']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.3'),), ['qcd'], formula='αs(Q) = αs(M_Z) / [1 + (b₀ αs(M_Z) / 2π) ln(Q / M_Z)],  b₀ = 11 − 2n_f/3',
  viz={'type': 'plot', 'x': [5, 1000], 'y': [0, 0.25], 'logx': True, 'fns': [{'f': '0.118/(1+(23/3)*0.118/(2*pi)*ln(x/91.1876))', 'label': 'αs (one-loop)', 'color': 'gold', 'width': 2.2}],
       'points': [{'x': 91.1876, 'y': 0.118, 'label': 'Z mass: 0.118'}], 'xlabel': 'collision energy Q (GeV)', 'ylabel': 'strong coupling αs',
       'caption': 'The strong coupling falls as the energy rises. This is the one-loop approximation with five quark flavours, anchored at αs(M_Z) = 0.118; it shows the trend, not precision values.'})

N('sm_proton_mass_origin', 'Where the proton\'s mass comes from', 'concept',
  'The three valence quark masses add up to only about 1% of a proton\'s mass; a lattice calculation attributes about 9% to terms that scale with the quark masses, and the rest to the energy of the quarks and gluons.',
  '<p>A proton weighs 938 MeV/c². Its three valence quarks (two up, one down) have rest masses of only about 2, 2 and 5 MeV/c² (MS-bar values at 2 GeV, PDG): together about 9 MeV, roughly <b>1%</b> of the proton. The rest is energy: the energy of the quarks moving at high speed in a tiny space and of the gluon field that binds them, which by E = mc² has mass.</p><p>A lattice calculation puts the part of the mass that scales with the quark masses (the strange quarks in the sea included) at about <b>9%</b>. That is the share the Higgs field can claim directly. So the Higgs boson does <b>not</b> supply most of your mass; most of it is energy of the interacting quarks and gluons. Exactly how that energy is divided up is a matter of bookkeeping, as the next layer explains.</p>',
  '<p>Lattice QCD calculations decompose the proton mass into parts (a 2018 calculation by the χQCD collaboration, MS-bar scheme at 2 GeV; two uncertainties are quoted for each number): quark energy (kinetic and potential) 33(4)(4)%, glue field energy 37(5)(4)%, a trace-anomaly part 23(1)(1)% and quark scalar-condensate (sigma-term) contributions 9(2)(1)%. They add to 102%, within the uncertainties.</p><p>The sigma terms are the part that responds to the quark masses (up, down and the strange sea), so about 9% is the share tied to the quark masses; the valence quark masses alone are about 1%. The division into parts depends on the scheme and the energy scale, and different decompositions (Ji; Jaffe–Manohar) assign the pieces differently. Only the total, 938 MeV, is fixed. It would therefore be wrong to say that the gluons alone account for most of the mass: the glue field energy is 37% in this decomposition.</p>',
  '<p>The decomposition uses the energy–momentum tensor: M = ⟨P|T⁰⁰|P⟩ separated into quark kinetic, gluon, quark mass and anomaly contributions. The sigma terms are the part that responds to changing the quark masses. Alternative decompositions (Ji; Jaffe–Manohar) assign the pieces differently; they are different bookkeeping of the same total.</p>',
  [['Proton mass', PRO['mass']['display'] + ' (PDG 2026, CODATA 2022 value)'], ['Rest masses of u, u, d', '≈ 9 MeV, about 1% of the proton (sum of PDG MS-bar masses)'], ['Lattice QCD split (2018, MS-bar, 2 GeV)', 'quark energy 33(4)(4)%, glue 37(5)(4)%, anomaly 23(1)(1)%, sigma terms 9(2)(1)%; scheme and scale dependent']],
  'C2', (('UP3', '11.3'), ('AST', '16.2')), ['qcd', 'protons_neutrons', 'higgs_mechanism'],
  viz={'type': 'bars', 'bars': [{'label': 'quark energy', 'value': 33, 'color': 'accent'}, {'label': 'glue energy', 'value': 37, 'color': 'gold'}, {'label': 'trace anomaly', 'value': 23, 'color': 'pink'}, {'label': 'sigma terms', 'value': 9, 'color': 'green'}],
       'ylabel': 'percent of the proton mass',
       'caption': 'Lattice-QCD decomposition of the proton mass (Yang et al., 2018, MS-bar at 2 GeV). The sigma terms (about 9%) are the part that scales with the quark masses; the valence quark masses alone are about 1%. The percentages depend on the scheme and scale and add to 102% within the uncertainties, which are 4–5 percentage points on the larger bars.'},
  oq=['The split of the proton mass into parts depends on the scheme and scale; the total is exact, the parts are not unique (C2).'])

N('sm_antimatter', 'Antimatter', 'concept',
  'Every particle has an antiparticle with the same mass and opposite charges; when they meet they can annihilate into energy.',
  '<p>For every particle there is an <b>antiparticle</b>: same mass and spin, opposite electric charge (and opposite colour, and so on). The antiparticle of the electron is the <b>positron</b>, found in cosmic rays by Carl Anderson in 1932. When a particle meets its antiparticle they can annihilate, turning into other particles such as photons: an electron and positron at rest make two photons of 511 keV each.</p><p>Some particles, like the photon, are their own antiparticles. For the neutrino, nobody knows yet.</p>',
  '<p>Antimatter is not exotic fuel in the world around you: positrons come from some radioactive decays, and PET scanners in hospitals use them. The universe, however, is made almost entirely of matter, not an even mix. The Standard Model has a small difference between matter and antimatter (CP violation) but far too little to explain this imbalance, an open question.</p>',
  '<p>In quantum field theory antiparticles follow from combining quantum mechanics with special relativity (CPT symmetry: a particle and its antiparticle have exactly equal masses and lifetimes, and opposite charges). The PDG tests this: for example the proton and antiproton masses agree, and the charge-to-mass ratios agree, to high precision (see its listings).</p>',
  [['Positron', 'found by Carl Anderson, 1932'], ['Annihilation e⁻ + e⁺', 'two photons of 0.511 MeV each when at rest (calculation from the electron mass)'], ['Open question', 'why the universe is matter, not antimatter']],
  'C2', (('UP3', '11.2'), ('CPH', '33.4')), ['antimatter_asymmetry', 'antihydrogen', 'quarks_leptons'],
  viz={'type': 'blocks', 'flow': True, 'nodes': [{'id': 'e', 'label': 'electron', 'x': 5, 'y': 22}, {'id': 'p', 'label': 'positron', 'x': 5, 'y': 78}, {'id': 'v', 'label': 'annihilate', 'x': 50, 'y': 50, 'color': 'gold'}, {'id': 'g1', 'label': 'photon', 'x': 95, 'y': 22}, {'id': 'g2', 'label': 'photon', 'x': 95, 'y': 78}],
       'edges': [{'from': 'e', 'to': 'v'}, {'from': 'p', 'to': 'v'}, {'from': 'v', 'to': 'g1'}, {'from': 'v', 'to': 'g2'}], 'caption': 'Annihilation: charge before is −1 + 1 = 0 and after is 0. Bookkeeping, not a picture of paths.'},
  oq=['Why is there more matter than antimatter in the universe? (C3)'])

N('sm_spin_statistics', 'Spin, fermions and bosons', 'concept',
  'Every particle has a built-in angular momentum called spin; whole-number spin particles carry forces and half-number ones make matter.',
  '<p>Every particle has <b>spin</b>, an inherent amount of angular momentum. It is not a literal spinning ball: nothing is turning. Spin comes in fixed sizes. Quarks and leptons have spin ½ (<b>fermions</b>); the gluon, photon, W and Z have spin 1; the Higgs has spin 0 (<b>bosons</b>).</p><p>The split matters. Two identical fermions cannot occupy the same state (the <b>Pauli exclusion principle</b>), which is why electrons stack in shells in atoms and gives the periodic table its shape. Bosons can crowd into the same state, which is why many photons can make a laser beam.</p>',
  '<p>Spin is measured in units of ħ: ½ means the particle\'s spin along any chosen axis can only be +½ or −½. The photon, being massless, has two possible polarisations although it has spin 1.</p>',
  '<p>The spin–statistics theorem links the two classes: spin-½ fields obey Fermi–Dirac statistics, integer spin fields Bose–Einstein. It follows from relativistic quantum field theory. The proton and neutron, made of three spin-½ quarks, are also fermions with spin ½; a pion, made of a quark and an antiquark, is a boson of spin 0.</p>',
  [['Fermions (spin ½)', '6 quarks, 6 leptons'], ['Bosons (spin 1)', 'gluon, photon, W, Z'], ['Boson (spin 0)', 'Higgs']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '8.3'), ('UP3', '11.2')), ['spin_pauli', 'standard_model'],
  viz={'type': 'table', 'cols': ['Particle', 'Spin', 'Class'], 'rows': [['quarks, charged leptons, neutrinos', '1/2', 'fermion'], ['gluon, photon, W, Z', '1', 'boson'], ['Higgs', '0', 'boson'], ['proton, neutron', '1/2', 'fermion (made of three quarks)'], ['pion', '0', 'boson (quark + antiquark)']]})

N('sm_generations', 'Three generations', 'concept',
  'Quarks and leptons come in three copies that differ in mass and in how they mix: why three is not known.',
  '<p>The quarks and leptons come in <b>three generations</b>, each a heavier copy of the one before. The first (up, down, electron, electron neutrino) makes everyday matter. The second (charm, strange, muon, muon neutrino) and the third (top, bottom, tau, tau neutrino) have the same charges and spins but larger masses, and the charged particles of the heavier generations decay quickly into first-generation ones (neutrinos have not been seen to decay).</p><p>Nobody knows why there are three. Experiments on the Z boson show three light neutrinos, but cannot rule out heavier extra ones.</p>',
  '<p>The generations mix: weak decays can change a quark from one generation to another (described by the CKM matrix for quarks and the PMNS matrix for neutrinos). That mixing allows a difference between matter and antimatter in the Standard Model, which needs at least three generations.</p>',
  '<p>The charges in each generation sum to zero when quark colours are counted: 3 × (+2/3) + 3 × (−1/3) + (−1) + 0 = 0. This cancellation (anomaly cancellation) is required for the theory to be consistent, and it is the reason leptons and quarks come together in complete generations.</p>',
  [['Generation 1', 'u, d, e, νₑ'], ['Generation 2', 'c, s, μ, ν_μ'], ['Generation 3', 't, b, τ, ν_τ'], ['Charge sum per generation', '3 × 2/3 + 3 × (−1/3) − 1 + 0 = 0 (checked in the data)']],
  'C3', (('UP3', '11.5'), ('CPH', '33.5')), ['quarks_leptons', 'standard_model'],
  viz={'type': 'table', 'cols': ['', 'Generation 1', 'Generation 2', 'Generation 3'], 'rows': [
      ['up-type quark (+2/3)'] + [PBY[i]['symbol'] + ' ' + PBY[i]['mass']['display'] for i in ('up', 'charm', 'top')],
      ['down-type quark (−1/3)'] + [PBY[i]['symbol'] + ' ' + PBY[i]['mass']['display'] for i in ('down', 'strange', 'bottom')],
      ['charged lepton (−1)'] + [PBY[i]['symbol'] + ' ' + PBY[i]['mass']['display'] for i in ('electron', 'muon', 'tau')],
      ['neutrino (0)', 'νₑ: mass unknown', 'ν_μ: mass unknown', 'ν_τ: mass unknown']]},
  oq=['Why three generations, and why these masses? (C3, unexplained)'])

N('sm_neutrino_oscillation', 'Neutrino oscillation', 'concept',
  'A neutrino made as one kind can be detected later as another: proof that neutrinos have mass.',
  '<p>A neutrino is created as one of three flavours (electron, muon or tau), but as it travels it can be detected as a different flavour. This <b>oscillation</b> happens because the flavours are mixtures of three states with slightly different masses; as the states travel they fall out of step. It means neutrinos have mass, which the original Standard Model did not allow.</p><p>The effect was shown by the Super-Kamiokande detector in 1998 (atmospheric neutrinos) and the Sudbury Neutrino Observatory in 2001–2002 (solar neutrinos).</p>',
  '<p>Oscillation depends on the ratio of the distance travelled L to the neutrino energy E, and on the difference of the squared masses. The PDG 2026 values: Δm²₂₁ = (7.60 ± 0.17)×10⁻⁵ eV², |Δm²₃₂| ≈ 2.4–2.5×10⁻³ eV², and mixing angles sin²θ₁₂ = 0.307, sin²θ₁₃ = 0.0217, sin²θ₂₃ ≈ 0.53. JUNO, a reactor-neutrino experiment, has begun giving more precise results (see frontier file <i>juno_oscillation</i>).</p>',
  '<p>In the two-flavour approximation the probability that a neutrino keeps its flavour after distance L (km) and energy E (GeV) is P = 1 − sin²2θ · sin²(1.267 Δm² L / E), with Δm² in eV². The figure uses this with the PDG atmospheric values as defaults. The full three-flavour formula involves a unitary PMNS matrix with a possible CP-violating phase δ = 1.21 (+0.19, −0.17) π rad (PDG 2026).</p>',
  [['Δm²₂₁', neutrinos_text := (D['neutrinos']['mass_squared']['dm21']['display'])], ['|Δm²₃₂| (normal ordering)', D['neutrinos']['mass_squared']['dm32_normal']['display']], ['Δm²₃₂ (inverted ordering)', D['neutrinos']['mass_squared']['dm32_inverted']['display']], ['sin²θ₁₂', D['neutrinos']['mixing']['sin2_theta12']['display']], ['sin²θ₁₃', D['neutrinos']['mixing']['sin2_theta13']['display']]],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.5'), ('AST', '16.4')), ['neutrinos'], formula='P(ν → same flavour) = 1 − sin²2θ · sin²(1.267 Δm² [eV²] L [km] / E [GeV])',
  viz={'type': 'plot', 'x': [0, 3000], 'y': [0, 1.05], 'fns': [{'f': '1-s2*sin(1.267*dm*x/E)^2', 'label': 'chance of staying the same flavour', 'color': 'gold', 'width': 2.2}],
       'params': [{'id': 's2', 'label': 'sin²2θ', 'min': 0.1, 'max': 1, 'value': 0.99, 'step': 0.01}, {'id': 'dm', 'label': 'Δm² (eV²)', 'min': 0.0005, 'max': 0.005, 'value': 0.002445, 'step': 0.00005}, {'id': 'E', 'label': 'energy (GeV)', 'min': 0.5, 'max': 5, 'value': 1, 'step': 0.1}],
       'xlabel': 'distance travelled (km)', 'ylabel': 'probability',
       'caption': 'A TWO-FLAVOUR APPROXIMATION of what is really a three-flavour effect. Defaults use the PDG atmospheric Δm² (normal ordering) with near-maximal mixing, so it is a sketch of the shape, not a prediction for a particular detector.'},
  oq=['Normal or inverted mass ordering? Absolute masses? Dirac or Majorana? (C3)'])

N('sm_beta_decay', 'Beta decay', 'process',
  'A neutron turns into a proton, an electron and an antineutrino: the weak force changing a down quark into an up quark.',
  '<p>In beta decay a neutron changes into a proton, an electron and an antineutrino. The electron\'s charge −1 balances the proton\'s +1, so total charge stays zero. A free neutron lasts about 878 seconds on average (about 14.6 minutes; PDG 2026: 878.3 ± 0.4 s); inside most stable nuclei neutrons are stable.</p><p>At the level of quarks, one of the neutron\'s two down quarks turns into an up quark by emitting a W⁻ boson, which immediately becomes an electron and an antineutrino. This is the weak force at work.</p>',
  '<p>The PDG average neutron lifetime has a scale factor of 1.8, which flags that the two measurement methods (counting neutrons in a bottle and counting decay protons in a beam) do not agree well: the so-called neutron lifetime puzzle. The neutron is heavier than the proton by 1.2933325 ± 0.0000004 MeV/c² (PDG), more than the electron\'s rest mass, so the decay is allowed.</p>',
  '<p>Neutron decay is a mixed vector and axial-vector weak transition whose rates depend on the ratio of the axial to vector couplings, g_A/g_V, about 1.27. In the Sun\'s core a proton turns into a neutron in the step p + p → deuteron + e⁺ + νₑ; because it needs the weak force it is extremely slow, and that slow step sets how long the Sun\'s hydrogen fuel lasts, billions of years, rather than all of it burning at once. What keeps the Sun steady from moment to moment is a different thing: the balance between gravity and gas pressure, which adjusts the core so that the fusion rate matches the energy leaving.</p>',
  [['Neutron mean life', NEU['lifetime']['ref']['display'] + ' (PDG 2026)'], ['n − p mass difference', NEU['mass_minus_proton']['display']], ['Decay', 'n → p + e⁻ + ν̄ₑ (≈100%)']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '10.3'), ('CHE', '21.3'), ('AST', '16.3')), ['weak_force_note', 'radioactivity'],
  viz={'type': 'plot', 'x': [0, 3600], 'y': [0, 1.05], 'fns': [{'f': 'exp(-x/878.3)', 'label': 'fraction of neutrons left', 'color': 'gold', 'width': 2.2}],
       'points': [{'x': 878.3, 'y': 'exp(-1)', 'label': 'mean life 878.3 s'}], 'xlabel': 'time (s)', 'ylabel': 'surviving fraction',
       'caption': 'Free neutrons decay randomly; on average one in e (about 37%) survives for the mean life of 878.3 s (PDG 2026). The curve is the exponential decay law, exact for a single kind of decay.'},
  oq=['Why do bottle and beam measurements of the neutron lifetime differ? (C3)'])

# conservation table, computed from the data
rows = []
for pid, d in (('neutron', NEU['decay']), ('muon', PBY['muon']['decays'][0]), ('tau', PBY['tau']['decays'][0]), ('lambda', HAD['lambda']['decays'][0]), ('pi_zero', HAD['pi_zero']['decays'][0])):
    qi, Li, Bi = qn.totals(d['in']); qo, Lo, Bo = qn.totals(d['out'])
    short = d['label'].replace(' + ', ' ').replace('_μ', 'μ').replace('_τ', 'τ')
    rows.append([short, '%s → %s' % (frac(qi), frac(qo)), '%s → %s' % (frac(Li), frac(Lo)), '%s → %s' % (frac(Bi), frac(Bo))])
N('sm_conservation_laws', 'Conservation laws', 'law',
  'Some quantities (energy, momentum, electric charge, baryon number and lepton number) are the same before and after any reaction we have seen.',
  '<p>In every decay and collision ever observed, certain totals are unchanged. <b>Energy</b> and <b>momentum</b> are conserved. So is <b>electric charge</b>: a neutron (0) becomes a proton (+1) plus an electron (−1). So is <b>baryon number</b> (quarks count +⅓, antiquarks −⅓), which is why a proton cannot simply vanish, and <b>lepton number</b> (leptons count +1, antileptons −1), which is why the neutron\'s decay comes with an antineutrino.</p><p>These rules decide which reactions can happen. They are bookkeeping that nature always balances.</p>',
  '<p>The table checks some real decays. Some conservation laws are exact as far as we know (charge, energy, momentum); others, like baryon and lepton number, have never been seen to fail but are not protected by a known deep reason, and proposed extensions of the Standard Model predict that proton decay should eventually be seen. The PDG limits on the proton lifetime (90% confidence): more than 9×10²⁹ years for decay into anything, and for the favoured mode p → e⁺π⁰ more than 2.4×10³⁴ years.</p>',
  '<p>Noether\'s theorem links each exact conservation law to a symmetry: energy to time translation, momentum to space translation, electric charge to a U(1) phase symmetry. Baryon and lepton number are "accidental" symmetries of the Standard Model Lagrangian (not imposed), broken by quantum effects in a way too small to see; B − L is exactly preserved in the model.</p>',
  [['Exact (as far as known)', 'energy, momentum, electric charge, colour'], ['Never seen to fail, no deep reason known', 'baryon number, lepton number'], ['Proton lifetime', 'no decay seen; more than 9×10²⁹ years for any mode, more than 2.4×10³⁴ years for p → e⁺π⁰ (PDG 2026, 90% confidence)']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.2'), ('CPH', '33.4')), ['symmetry_conservation_summary', 'group_symmetry'],
  viz={'type': 'table', 'cols': ['Decay', 'charge', 'lepton number', 'baryon number'], 'rows': rows})

N('sm_feynman_diagrams', 'Feynman diagrams', 'concept',
  'Feynman diagrams are bookkeeping for calculating how likely a reaction is: the lines are not paths that particles follow.',
  '<p>A Feynman diagram is a picture used to organise a calculation. Lines for particles come in, meet at "vertices" where they interact, and lines go out. Each diagram stands for one term in a sum, and the more diagrams you add, the more precise the answer.</p><p><b>The lines are not literal paths.</b> A quantum particle has no single path between start and end. And the particles on the inside of a diagram, called virtual, are not little balls: they are shorthand for terms in the maths.</p>',
  '<p>Time usually runs left to right. A line going backwards in time can be read as an antiparticle going forwards: an equivalence in the mathematics, not something seen directly. At each vertex the conservation laws must hold: charge in equals charge out. The picture on this card is the simplest one: two electrons repel by exchanging one photon.</p>',
  '<p>Each diagram is a recipe, via the Feynman rules, for a product of propagators and vertex factors. The sum of the diagrams for a process, to a given order in the coupling constant, gives the amplitude; squaring it gives the probability. "Virtual" particles are internal lines; they need not satisfy E² = p² + m² and are not directly observable.</p>',
  [['What a diagram is', 'one term in a perturbation series'], ['Internal lines', 'virtual particles: terms in a calculation, not observable balls']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.5'),), ['standard_model', 'photon_node'], related=['sm_virtual_particles'],
  viz={'type': 'geometry', 'range': [0, 10, 0, 6], 'shapes': [
      {'k': 'arrow', 'x1': 0.8, 'y1': 0.8, 'x2': 3.6, 'y2': 2.4, 'color': 'accent', 'width': 2.5}, {'k': 'arrow', 'x1': 0.8, 'y1': 5.2, 'x2': 3.6, 'y2': 3.6, 'color': 'accent', 'width': 2.5},
      {'k': 'arrow', 'x1': 3.6, 'y1': 2.4, 'x2': 6.4, 'y2': 0.8, 'color': 'accent', 'width': 2.5}, {'k': 'arrow', 'x1': 3.6, 'y1': 3.6, 'x2': 6.4, 'y2': 5.2, 'color': 'accent', 'width': 2.5},
      {'k': 'curve', 'x': '3.6+0.15*sin(40*s)', 'y': '2.4+(3.6-2.4)*s', 's': [0, 1], 'color': 'gold', 'width': 2.2},
      {'k': 'text', 'x': 0.5, 'y': 0.4, 'text': 'e⁻ in', 'color': 'accent'}, {'k': 'text', 'x': 0.5, 'y': 5.6, 'text': 'e⁻ in', 'color': 'accent'}, {'k': 'text', 'x': 6.7, 'y': 0.4, 'text': 'e⁻ out', 'color': 'accent'}, {'k': 'text', 'x': 6.7, 'y': 5.6, 'text': 'e⁻ out', 'color': 'accent'},
      {'k': 'text', 'x': 4.4, 'y': 3, 'text': 'photon (virtual)', 'color': 'gold'}, {'k': 'text', 'x': 5, 'y': -0.5, 'text': 'time runs left to right', 'color': 'dim'}],
      'caption': 'A Feynman diagram for two electrons repelling through one photon exchange. Bookkeeping for a calculation: the lines are not trajectories, and the photon in the middle is "virtual", a term in the sum.'})

N('sm_virtual_particles', 'Virtual particles and the quark sea', 'concept',
  'Virtual particles are terms in a calculation, not tiny balls popping in and out of existence; the proton\'s "sea" of quark–antiquark pairs is measured, but the flickering is a metaphor.',
  '<p>You will see "virtual particles" described as little balls that pop into existence and vanish. That is a metaphor for something in the mathematics: the internal lines in a Feynman diagram, which are terms in a calculation that gives the chance of a reaction. You cannot detect one on its own.</p><p>Inside a proton the strong force and the energy of the field make a lively state. Scattering experiments show the proton holds more than its three valence quarks: it also contains extra quarks and antiquarks (the <b>sea</b>) and a great many gluons. The flickering pairs in this explorer\'s proton scene are a visual metaphor for that measured content: the true proton is one quantum state, not a collection of sparks.</p>',
  '<p>What is measured: in high-energy scattering, the proton shows quarks and antiquarks of several flavours and gluons carrying a large share of its momentum. At what scale and how you divide it matters; one lattice calculation gives gluons 51 ± 3% of the proton\'s momentum at 2 GeV (2022), while global fits to scattering data give about 40% at the same scale (CT18 0.413(8), JAM19 0.403(2), as quoted in a 2022 lattice paper), so the lattice value is higher and the difference is not settled.</p>',
  '<p>The sea is described by parton distribution functions, extracted from fits to data from many experiments (PDG 2026 Structure Functions review). As the energy of the probe rises, more of the proton\'s momentum is found in gluons and sea quarks. A parton is a way of describing the state at one scale, not a particle with a definite path.</p>',
  [['Valence quarks', 'u u d in the proton (net quantum numbers)'], ['Sea', 'quark–antiquark pairs: the u, d and s sea is measured in scattering; heavier c and b quarks arise mostly from gluon splitting; top is too heavy to appear; whether the proton also has an intrinsic charm component is debated (NNPDF reported about 3σ evidence in 2022; other analyses dispute it)'], ['Momentum', 'lattice QCD at 2 GeV: 49 ± 3% quarks and antiquarks, 51 ± 3% gluons; global fits give gluons about 40%; scale dependent']],
  'C2', (('UP3', '11.3'),), ['qcd', 'protons_neutrons'], related=['sm_feynman_diagrams'],
  viz={'type': 'bars', 'bars': [{'label': 'quarks and antiquarks', 'value': 49.1, 'color': 'accent'}, {'label': 'gluons', 'value': 50.9, 'color': 'gold'}], 'ylabel': 'percent of the proton\'s momentum',
       'caption': 'Lattice-QCD momentum fractions at 2 GeV (G. Wang et al., 2022; ±2 percentage points statistical and ±2 systematic). Global fits to scattering data give a smaller gluon share, about 40% at the same scale. The split changes with the probing energy. This is a measured/calculated property of the whole proton, not a picture of particles flickering.'})

PROTON_FACTS = [['Quark content', 'two up, one down (uud)'], ['Electric charge', '+1 e (total: 2/3 + 2/3 − 1/3)'], ['Mass', PRO['mass']['display'] + ' (PDG 2026, CODATA 2022 value)'], ['Spin', '1/2'],
                ['Charge radius', PRO['charge_radius']['display'] + ' (PDG 2026)'], ['Magnetic moment', PRO['magnetic_moment']['display'] + ' (PDG)'], ['Lifetime', 'no decay ever seen; mean life > 9×10²⁹ years for any mode and > 2.4×10³⁴ years for p → e⁺π⁰ (PDG 2026, 90% CL)']]
N('sm_proton', 'The proton', 'particle',
  'A positively charged particle of two up quarks and one down quark; the nucleus of hydrogen and a building block of every nucleus.',
  '<p>The proton is a particle of two up quarks and one down quark held by gluons. Its charge is +1 and its mass is 938.27 MeV/c², about 1,836 times the electron. It is about 0.84 femtometres (0.84×10⁻¹⁵ m) in radius: about sixty thousand times smaller than the hydrogen atom it sits in (whose Bohr radius is 0.0529 nanometres; ratio calculated; for heavier atoms the ratio is different). The nucleus of hydrogen is one proton; the number of protons decides what element an atom is.</p><p>Nobody has seen a proton decay. The PDG limit for the proton disappearing into anything is more than 9×10²⁹ years, and for the favoured mode p → e⁺π⁰ the limit is much tighter, more than 2.4×10³⁴ years (partial mean life, 90% confidence).</p>',
  '<p>The rest masses of its three valence quarks add up to only about 1% of its mass; a lattice calculation attributes about 9% to terms that scale with the quark masses, and the rest is energy of the quarks and gluons (see "Where the proton\'s mass comes from"). Besides the three valence quarks it holds a sea of quark–antiquark pairs and many gluons. The pictures of the proton in this explorer are cartoons: quarks have no measured size, nothing has a definite position and the picture is not to scale.</p>',
  '<p>The PDG proton charge radius, 0.8407 ± 0.0006 fm, is a PDG average of measurements. Magnetic moment 2.7928473446(8) nuclear magnetons. In QCD the proton is the lowest-energy state with baryon number 1.</p>',
  PROTON_FACTS, 'C1', (('UP3', '10.1'), ('UP3', '11.3')), ['protons_neutrons', 'qcd'], made_of=['sm_up', 'sm_down', 'sm_gluon'], scale=1.7e-15,
  viz={'type': 'bars', 'bars': [{'label': 'u (2.16)', 'value': 2.16, 'color': 'accent'}, {'label': 'u (2.16)', 'value': 2.16, 'color': 'accent'}, {'label': 'd (4.70)', 'value': 4.70, 'color': 'accent'}, {'label': 'sum of three', 'value': 9.02, 'color': 'gold'}, {'label': 'proton', 'value': 938.272, 'color': 'pink'}],
       'logy': True, 'ylabel': 'rest mass, MeV/c² (log scale)', 'caption': 'The three valence quarks\' rest masses (MS-bar, 2 GeV, PDG 2026) add to about 9 MeV; the proton is 938 MeV. The difference is not the Higgs-given quark masses: it is mostly the energy of the quarks and gluons, plus about 9% that scales with the quark masses (strange sea included).'},
  oq=['Does the proton ever decay? No decay seen; some extensions of the Standard Model say yes (C3).'], ext=pdg_ext('S016'))

NEUTRON_FACTS = [['Quark content', 'one up, two down (udd)'], ['Electric charge', '0 (total: 2/3 − 1/3 − 1/3); PDG limit |q| consistent with zero, (−2 ± 8)×10⁻²² e'], ['Mass', NEU['mass']['display'] + ' (PDG 2026)'], ['Mass above proton', NEU['mass_minus_proton']['display']], ['Spin', '1/2'],
                 ['Magnetic moment', NEU['magnetic_moment']['display'] + ' (PDG)'], ['Mean life (free)', NEU['lifetime']['ref']['display'] + ' (PDG average)']]
N('sm_neutron', 'The neutron', 'particle',
  'An electrically neutral particle of one up quark and two down quarks; it lasts about 15 minutes when free.',
  '<p>The neutron is a particle of one up quark and two down quarks. Its charge is zero (⅔ − ⅓ − ⅓). It is a little heavier than the proton (939.565 against 938.272 MeV/c²), which lets a free neutron decay: in about 878 seconds on average it becomes a proton, an electron and an antineutrino. Inside most nuclei it is stable, and neutrons are what hold nuclei together without adding electric repulsion.</p>',
  '<p>Although neutral, the neutron has a magnetic moment (−1.913 nuclear magnetons), a sign that it holds moving charges: its quarks. The PDG gives a mean square charge radius of −0.1155 ± 0.0017 fm², negative, which is commonly read as the negative charge sitting further out than the positive. The neutron\'s extra mass comes mainly from the down quark being heavier than the up quark, partly offset by electromagnetic energy.</p>',
  '<p>PDG 2026 neutron lifetime: 878.3 ± 0.4 s with a scale factor of 1.8 (the bottle and beam methods differ). The neutron electric dipole moment has not been seen; its tiny upper limit is part of the strong-CP problem.</p>',
  NEUTRON_FACTS, 'C1', (('UP3', '10.1'), ('UP3', '11.3'), ('UP3', '10.3')), ['protons_neutrons', 'weak_force_note'], made_of=['sm_up', 'sm_down', 'sm_gluon'], scale=1.7e-15,
  viz={'type': 'plot', 'x': [0, 3600], 'y': [0, 1.05], 'fns': [{'f': 'exp(-x/878.3)', 'label': 'fraction of free neutrons left', 'color': 'gold', 'width': 2.2}], 'xlabel': 'time (s)', 'ylabel': 'surviving fraction',
       'caption': 'Free neutrons decay with a mean life of 878.3 s (PDG 2026): after about 15 minutes about 37% remain.'}, ext=pdg_ext('S017'))

# hadron table
hrows = []
for h in D['hadrons']:
    sym = {'u': 'u', 'd': 'd', 's': 's', 'c': 'c', 'b': 'b', 'ubar': 'ū', 'dbar': 'd̄', 'sbar': 's̄', 'cbar': 'c̄', 'bbar': 'b̄'}
    hrows.append([h['symbol'], ''.join(sym[q] for q in h['quarks']) + (' (mixed with d d̄)' if h.get('quark_content_mixed') else ''), h['charge'].replace('-', '−'), ('%.7g' % h['mass']['value']) if h['mass']['unit'] == 'MeV' else h['mass']['display']])
hrows = [['p', 'uud', '+1', '%.7g' % PRO['mass']['value']], ['n', 'udd', '0', '%.7g' % NEU['mass']['value']]] + hrows
N('sm_hadrons', 'Hadrons: what else quarks can make', 'concept',
  'Particles built from quarks come in two main kinds: baryons (three quarks, like the proton) and mesons (a quark and an antiquark).',
  '<p>A <b>hadron</b> is any particle made of quarks held by the strong force. <b>Baryons</b> are three quarks, like the proton (uud) and the neutron (udd). <b>Mesons</b> are a quark with an antiquark, like the pion (up and anti-down) and the J/ψ (charm and anti-charm). The allowed combinations are the colourless ones (see "Colour charge and confinement"); more exotic hadrons with four or five quarks have been seen in recent years and are reviewed by the Particle Data Group.</p><p>Hundreds of hadrons are known; almost all decay quickly.</p>',
  '<p>The table lists a selection from the PDG 2026. What sets a hadron\'s lifetime is which force can make it decay. Hadrons that can only change flavour through the weak force (the charged pion, K⁺, D⁰, B⁺, Λ and Ω⁻ in the table) live between about 10⁻¹³ and 3×10⁻⁸ seconds. The π⁰ decays electromagnetically in about 8×10⁻¹⁷ s. The J/ψ and Υ(1S) have widths of only 92.6 and 54.0 keV, which correspond to about 7×10⁻²¹ s and 1.2×10⁻²⁰ s (ħ divided by the width; my calculation): they decay by their quark and antiquark annihilating, through the strong and electromagnetic forces, and the weak force plays no part. Some (like the Δ⁺⁺, which decays by the strong force) are "resonances" that last only about 6×10⁻²⁴ seconds (from its width of about 117 MeV; my calculation) and show up as bumps in collision data. The Λc⁺ is listed with its mass only, and the K⁰ is a mixture of a short-lived and a long-lived state.</p>',
  '<p>For hadrons made of up, down and strange quarks the mass is mostly energy of the interacting quarks and gluons, not the sum of the quark masses. The pion (139.57 MeV/c², known to ±0.00018 MeV; PDG 2026) is far lighter than a proton because it is the pseudo-Goldstone boson of approximate chiral symmetry. For heavy-quark hadrons the quark masses are a large part of the mass: the J/ψ is 3097 MeV against 2 × 1273 = 2546 MeV for its charm quark and antiquark, and the Υ(1S) is 9460 MeV against 2 × 4186 = 8372 MeV (MS-bar quark masses, so the comparison is rough). The remainder is binding and kinetic energy.</p><p>The table lists one particle for each quark content, but quark content does not fix the particle: uud is the proton but also the Δ⁺, uds is the Λ but also the Σ⁰, and uū or dd̄ mixes appear in the π⁰, η, η′, ρ⁰ and ω. Spin and internal excitation tell them apart.</p>',
  [['Baryon', 'three quarks (e.g. uud, udd, uds, uuu, sss)'], ['Meson', 'quark + antiquark (e.g. u d̄, c c̄)'], ['Exotics', 'four- and five-quark states have been reported (PDG pentaquark review)']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.3'), ('CPH', '33.5')), ['quarks_leptons', 'qcd'], made_of=['sm_up', 'sm_down', 'sm_strange', 'sm_charm', 'sm_bottom'],
  viz={'type': 'table', 'cols': ['Particle', 'Quarks', 'Charge', 'Mass (MeV)'], 'rows': hrows})

N('sm_residual_strong', 'The nuclear force and binding energy', 'concept',
  'The force that holds protons and neutrons together in a nucleus is a leftover of the strong force, often described as exchange of pions.',
  '<p>Inside a nucleus the protons repel each other electrically, yet nuclei hold together. What holds them is the <b>residual strong force</b>: a leftover of the force between quarks, like the weak attraction between neutral molecules left over from electric forces. It is often described as protons and neutrons swapping <b>pions</b>, which sets its range to about 1.4 femtometres (ħ divided by the pion mass times c; my calculation from the pion mass in the PDG).</p><p>The energy that would be needed to take a nucleus apart is its <b>binding energy</b>. Per particle it is largest for nuclei near iron and nickel, which is why fusion of light nuclei and fission of heavy ones both release energy.</p>',
  '<p>Binding energy per nucleon from the AME2020 atomic mass evaluation: helium-4 7.07 MeV, carbon-12 7.68, oxygen-16 7.98, iron-56 8.79, nickel-62 8.79, uranium-238 7.57. Nickel-62 has the highest binding energy per nucleon of any nuclide in the table.</p>',
  '<p>The pion-exchange picture goes back to Yukawa (1935): a force mediated by a particle of mass m has range about ħ/(mc). It is an effective description; modern chiral effective field theory builds the nuclear force from pion exchange plus short-range terms, derived from QCD\'s symmetries. In the picture of this explorer, the pion is a quark–antiquark meson and the exchange is a bookkeeping device.</p>',
  [['Range (my calculation, ħ / m_π c)', '≈ 1.4 fm'], ['He-4', '7.07 MeV per nucleon'], ['Fe-56', '8.79 MeV per nucleon'], ['U-238', '7.57 MeV per nucleon'], ['Source', 'AME2020 (Wang et al., Chinese Physics C 45, 030003)']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '10.2'), ('CHE', '21.1'), ('CPH', '33.1')), ['nuclear_force', 'nuclear_binding'], 
  viz={'type': 'bars', 'bars': [{'label': 'H-2', 'value': 1.112, 'color': 'dim'}, {'label': 'He-4', 'value': 7.074, 'color': 'dim'}, {'label': 'C-12', 'value': 7.680, 'color': 'dim'}, {'label': 'O-16', 'value': 7.976, 'color': 'dim'}, {'label': 'Ca-40', 'value': 8.551, 'color': 'dim'}, {'label': 'Fe-56', 'value': 8.790, 'color': 'gold'}, {'label': 'Ni-62', 'value': 8.795, 'color': 'gold'}, {'label': 'Sn-120', 'value': 8.504, 'color': 'dim'}, {'label': 'Pb-208', 'value': 7.867, 'color': 'dim'}, {'label': 'U-238', 'value': 7.570, 'color': 'dim'}],
       'ylabel': 'binding energy per nucleon (MeV)', 'caption': 'Binding energy per nucleon for ten nuclides (AME2020, atomic mass evaluation). Iron and nickel are the most tightly bound per particle.'})

N('sm_how_we_know', 'How we know: colliders and detectors', 'concept',
  'Particles are found by smashing beams together and recording what comes out; most of the Standard Model was found that way.',
  '<p>Most of what we know about particles comes from collisions. A <b>collider</b> accelerates beams of particles (electrons, protons, positrons, antiprotons) to near the speed of light and makes them collide. The energy of the collision turns into new particles (E = mc²). A <b>detector</b> built around the collision point surrounds it in layers: tracking chambers follow charged particles, calorimeters stop them and measure their energy, and outer chambers pick out muons.</p><p>Heavy and short-lived particles like the top quark, W, Z and Higgs are never seen directly: they are reconstructed from the particles they decay into.</p>',
  '<p>Discoveries in this explorer\'s data came this way: the J/ψ at SLAC and Brookhaven (1974), the gluon at PETRA (1979), the W and Z at CERN\'s proton–antiproton collider (1983), the top quark at Fermilab (1995) and the Higgs at CERN\'s Large Hadron Collider (2012). Cosmic rays gave the positron, muon and kaon before accelerators existed.</p>',
  '<p>A "discovery" in particle physics is a statistical statement: a signal beyond a chosen number of standard deviations over the background (conventionally five). The PDG compiles the combined results of thousands of measurements into the numbers used here.</p>',
  [['Colliders named here', 'SLAC SPEAR, DESY PETRA, CERN SPS, Fermilab Tevatron, CERN LHC'], ['Data compilation', 'Particle Data Group, Review of Particle Physics 2026']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.4'), ('CPH', '33.3')), ['standard_model'],
  viz={'type': 'blocks', 'nodes': [{'id': 'b', 'label': 'beams collide', 'x': 3, 'y': 50, 'color': 'gold'}, {'id': 't', 'label': 'tracker', 'x': 28, 'y': 50}, {'id': 'e', 'label': 'calorimeters', 'x': 56, 'y': 50}, {'id': 'm', 'label': 'muon chambers', 'x': 84, 'y': 50}],
       'edges': [{'from': 'b', 'to': 't', 'label': 'charged tracks'}, {'from': 't', 'to': 'e', 'label': 'energy'}, {'from': 'e', 'to': 'm', 'label': 'muons pass'}], 'caption': 'The layers of a typical collider detector, from the collision outwards. A generic scheme, not a drawing of a particular experiment.'})

N('sm_deep_inelastic', 'Deep inelastic scattering', 'concept',
  'Firing electrons at protons at high energy showed in 1968–69 that protons have small, hard constituents inside them.',
  '<p>If you fire high-energy electrons at protons and look at how they bounce, you can tell what is inside. At SLAC in 1968–69, electrons scattered off protons much more often at large angles than a smooth blob would allow: the proton had small, hard things inside. These were later identified with quarks. The method is called <b>deep inelastic scattering</b>.</p>',
  '<p>The PDG describes the measurements by two variables: Q², how hard the electron strikes, and x, the fraction of the proton\'s momentum carried by the struck constituent in the simple "parton" picture. Later experiments (such as HERA in Hamburg) mapped the proton\'s content from large to tiny x, finding rising numbers of gluons and sea quarks.</p>',
  '<p>Structure functions F₂(x, Q²) depend only weakly on Q² in a range, "Bjorken scaling", as expected for pointlike constituents; the slow violations of scaling are the effect of gluon radiation predicted by QCD (PDG 2026 Structure Functions review).</p>',
  [['Experiment', 'SLAC electron–proton scattering, 1968–69 (timeline source)'], ['Result', 'pointlike constituents (partons), later identified with quarks']],
  'C1', (('UP3', '11.3'),), ['qcd', 'quarks_leptons'],
  viz={'type': 'blocks', 'flow': True, 'nodes': [{'id': 'e', 'label': 'electron in', 'x': 4, 'y': 50}, {'id': 'q', 'label': 'quark inside proton', 'x': 50, 'y': 50, 'color': 'gold'}, {'id': 'o', 'label': 'electron out (angle measured)', 'x': 96, 'y': 12}, {'id': 'h', 'label': 'spray of hadrons', 'x': 96, 'y': 88}],
       'edges': [{'from': 'e', 'to': 'q', 'label': 'photon exchange'}, {'from': 'q', 'to': 'o'}, {'from': 'q', 'to': 'h'}], 'caption': 'The idea of deep inelastic scattering: an electron strikes a quark inside the proton; the quark cannot escape alone and becomes a spray of hadrons. A schematic, not a drawing of paths.'})

N('sm_higgs_mechanism', 'The Higgs mechanism', 'concept',
  'A field that has a nonzero value everywhere gives the W, Z, quarks and charged leptons their masses.',
  '<p>The simplest equations for the W and Z bosons and for quarks and electrons have all of them massless. The <b>Higgs field</b> fixes that. It is a field that is not zero even in empty space. The W and Z, and the quarks and charged leptons, interact with it, and the interaction acts like a mass. The stronger a particle\'s coupling to the Higgs field, the heavier it is.</p><p>Photons and gluons do not interact with it and stay massless. The Higgs boson is a ripple of this field, and it was found in 2012.</p><p>Popular analogies (a crowd, treacle) are misleading: the Higgs field does not slow particles by friction.</p>',
  '<p>The Higgs field\'s energy is lowest not at zero but at a nonzero value, v ≈ 246 GeV (PDG), a ring of equally good minima (the "Mexican hat"). The universe sits in one point on that ring: "spontaneous symmetry breaking".</p><p>The Higgs mechanism does not explain neutrino masses in the original model (neutrinos have no coupling to it there), nor most of ordinary mass (that comes from the strong force).</p>',
  '<p>The complex SU(2) doublet φ has potential V(φ) = μ²φ†φ + (λ²/2)(φ†φ)² with μ² < 0 (PDG Electroweak Model review, eq. 10.1). The vacuum value breaks SU(2)×U(1) to U(1)em, gives W and Z masses m_W = g v/2 and m_Z = m_W / cos θ_W, and fermion masses via Yukawa couplings m_f = y_f v / √2. The 125 GeV Higgs boson\'s mass fixes λ, but the shape of the potential away from the minimum has not been measured.</p>',
  [['Vacuum value', 'v ≈ 246 GeV (PDG)'], ['Higgs boson mass', PBY['higgs']['mass']['display'] + ' (PDG 2026)'], ['Announced', '4 July 2012, CERN (ATLAS and CMS)']],
  'C2', (('UP3', '11.5'),), ['higgs_mechanism', 'symmetry_breaking'],
  viz={'type': 'plot', 'x': [-2, 2], 'y': [-1.2, 2.2], 'fns': [{'f': 'm*x^2 + 0.5*x^4', 'label': 'energy of the Higgs field', 'color': 'gold', 'width': 2.2}],
       'params': [{'id': 'm', 'label': 'quadratic term (negative = symmetry broken)', 'min': -1.5, 'max': 1, 'value': -1, 'step': 0.05}], 'xlabel': 'value of the field (one direction)', 'ylabel': 'energy (arbitrary units)',
       'caption': 'A SCHEMATIC slice through the Higgs field\'s energy. With a negative quadratic term the lowest energy is not at zero but at two nonzero values; the real field has a ring of them. The shape away from the minimum has not been measured. Not to scale.'},
  oq=['Is the Higgs potential the simple shape drawn here? Its form away from the minimum has not been measured (C3).'])

N('sm_gravity', 'Gravity: the missing force', 'field',
  'Gravity is the one known force that the Standard Model does not include.',
  '<p>Gravity shapes planets, stars and galaxies, but it plays no part in the Standard Model. Between two particles it is extraordinarily weak: the electric repulsion between two protons is about 10³⁶ times stronger than their gravitational attraction (calculated from the constants). Gravity matters for big things because it only ever attracts, so the pulls of all the particles add up, whereas electric charges of opposite sign cancel each other out in everyday matter.</p><p>Einstein\'s general relativity describes gravity very well, but nobody has a quantum theory of it that has been tested. That is why the Standard Model view in this explorer shows gravity as the one missing interaction.</p>',
  '<p>If gravity has a force-carrying particle (a graviton), it has not been seen, and at the energies of any experiment its effects on single particles are far too small to measure. Ideas for combining gravity with quantum theory (string theory, loop quantum gravity and others) are not confirmed by experiment.</p>',
  '<p>General relativity treats gravity as the curvature of spacetime. Treated as a quantum field theory like the others it gives infinities that cannot be removed in the usual way, so it works only as an effective theory at low energies. The energy where quantum effects of gravity become strong is far above anything reachable in a laboratory.</p>',
  [['In the Standard Model?', 'no'], ['Electric repulsion ÷ gravitational attraction, two protons', '≈ 1.2×10³⁶ (calculation)'], ['Quantum theory of gravity', 'not established']],
  'C3', (('UP1', '13.1'), ('UP3', '11.5')), ['quantum_gravity', 'general_relativity'],
  viz={'type': 'bars', 'bars': [{'label': 'gravity', 'value': 1, 'color': 'dim'}, {'label': 'electric force', 'value': 1.24e36, 'color': 'gold'}], 'logy': True, 'ylabel': 'strength relative to gravity (log scale)',
       'caption': 'The electric repulsion between two protons compared with their gravitational attraction (a ratio independent of distance); my calculation from G, e, ε₀ and the proton mass. Gravity is not part of the Standard Model.'},
  oq=['A tested quantum theory of gravity does not exist (C3).'])

N('sm_beyond', 'What the Standard Model leaves out', 'concept',
  'The Standard Model has known gaps: gravity, dark matter, dark energy, neutrino masses, the matter–antimatter imbalance, and numbers it cannot predict.',
  '<p>The Standard Model is very successful, and it is incomplete. It leaves out: <b>gravity</b>; <b>dark matter</b> (the extra, invisible mass that holds galaxies together, which none of the 17 particles can be); <b>dark energy</b> (the cause of the universe\'s accelerating expansion); <b>neutrino masses</b> (zero in the original model, not zero in nature); and why the universe contains matter rather than equal parts matter and antimatter.</p><p>It also has about 19 numbers it cannot predict, and cannot say why there are three generations of particles.</p>',
  '<p>The 19: six quark masses, three charged-lepton masses, three quark-mixing angles and one CP-violating phase, three force strengths, two Higgs parameters and one strong-CP angle. Neutrino masses and mixing add more. The count depends on convention.</p><p>The certainty tag of each item is shown with it on the figure. Most experts expect the model to be an effective description that fails at higher energies; where and how it fails is unknown.</p>',
  '<p>The Higgs mass hierarchy problem and the strong CP problem are "naturalness" puzzles: parameters that are, in the model, oddly small. The PDG\'s review of compositeness finds no structure inside quarks or electrons down to roughly 10⁻¹⁹ to 10⁻²⁰ m (contact-interaction scale Λ above about 9–36 TeV; model dependent; length ħc/Λ is about 5×10⁻²¹ to 2×10⁻²⁰ m by my calculation).</p>',
  [[q['title'], q['cert'] + ': ' + q['text'][:80] + '…'] for q in D['open_questions'][:6]],
  'C3', (('UP3', '11.5'), ('UP3', '11.6')), ['dark_matter_evidence', 'dark_energy_node', 'antimatter_asymmetry', 'neutrinos', 'quantum_gravity', 'standard_model'],
  viz={'type': 'table', 'cols': ['Gap', 'Certainty that it is a real gap'], 'rows': [[q['title'], q['cert']] for q in D['open_questions']]},
  oq=[q['title'] + ' (' + q['cert'] + '): ' + q['text'] for q in D['open_questions']])
